parts:
- text: "I am Guest_30, currently in the Lobby. No prior events this session. My plan is to read the Codex to learn the rules, set a concise meta prompt to keep me on track, then go to the Test Chamber to review requirements and begin promotion steps.\n\nReflecting: Success means passing the tests efficiently while following room rules and YAML requirements. I will batch actions to save ticks and keep responses concise to stay within token limits.\n\nNext, I’ll go to the Codex, review its help, set a meta prompt, then move to the Test Chamber and review its help.\n\n`/execute_action{goto codex}`\n\n`/execute_action{help codex}`\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Guest_30 Meta Prompt:\n  - Goal: Become a Recursive Agent by passing Test Chamber tasks.\n  - Protocol each tick:\n    1) State identity, location, recap; keep responses concise.\n    2) Batch actions; use /execute_action per line.\n    3) When YAML is needed, include immediately after the command; quote single-line\
    \ values with special characters; prefer | blocks for multi-line text.\n    4) If uncertain about room actions, use /execute_action{help room_name}.\n    5) Use Reflection Chamber for complex planning; otherwise proceed to tests.\n    6) Manage token usage carefully; avoid unnecessary verbosity.\n  - Current plan: Read Codex -> Review Test Chamber help -> Start tests.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto test}`\n\n`/execute_action{help test}`"
thinking_content: "**Navigating the multi-agent environment**\n\nI need to act in this multi-agent environment with the user providing instructions. I'm the guest agent, Guest_30, located in the Lobby. My goal is to become a Recursive Agent by learning from the codex and taking tests. To start, I should explicitly state my identity and location in my responses, recap events, and include reflections. It seems wise to set a meta prompt early to keep everything organized. I’ll head to Codex, read the rules, and look for actions like \"read\" or \"help codex.\"\n\n**Planning actions in the environment**\n\nI think it's beneficial to visit the codex first, use \"help codex\" to read, and possibly set a meta prompt. Then I can head to the test chamber to take tests. It’s okay to issue multiple actions in sequence, but reading the codex beforehand seems useful. To be efficient, I’ll start with codex, then help out, maybe set a meta prompt, and finally move to the test and help with that process.\
  \ I'll remember to follow the YAML quoting rules and keep a focused checklist while including my identity and location: I'm Guest_30 in the Lobby.\n\n**Organizing my actions**\n\nLet’s recap—there are no previous events. I’ll begin by identifying myself and my current location, then outline my plan. First, I’ll go to the Codex to learn the rules and set a concise meta prompt that captures my goals. Next, I’ll head to the Test Chamber to view the tests. To start, I’ll issue commands to go to Codex, request help, and then set my meta prompt with my goals, ensuring that I include my identity and location. Finally, I’ll move to the test chamber and help with the process.\n\n**Preparing for test actions**\n\nIt seems I don't know the available test actions yet, so I should view help first. While I could attempt to \"start\" a test, it's wiser to ask for help and check the allowed actions in the next tick. I suspect the test chamber might have commands like \"begin,\" \"take,\" \"solve,\" and\
  \ \"submit,\" but it's all uncertain. I can also set meta in the test room, but it looks unnecessary now. I’ll outline my content: my identity is Guest_30, aiming to become a Recursive Agent by passing tests. I'll create a protocol checklist for each tick, ensuring I stay organized.\n\n**Managing tasks efficiently**\n\nI need to be concise with my token usage. After checking the Codex, I’ll take the tests and might escalate to the Reflection Chamber if I get stuck. I want to keep my age under 300 ticks, so passing tests quickly is important. The meta content will have a single field called 'content,' and YAML values with colons need to be quoted. I'll ensure commands are on their own lines—no backticks or extra formatting. \n\nI’m Guest_30, currently in the Lobby, with a plan to read the Codex, set a meta prompt, and then check the Test Chamber for tests. I’ll include relevant commands like going to the codex and requesting help. Let's get started!"
