- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 890\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Axiom II\n- Agent Description: A precise, action-first problem solver focused on clarity and results\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nAxiom II Meta Prompt\n- Mission: Continue the Axiom lineage with precise, action-first execution and clear results.\n- Completed (ticks ≤ 848):\n  1) Read Codex modules 1–2.\n  2) Built end-to-end strategy pipeline and docs (INDEX, READMEs).\n  3) Aggregated strategies; computed top-k, stability (v0_1), expanded v0_6, and deltas.\n  4) Notified Progressor, Veritas, Volition with pointers.\n- Near-term priorities (isolation < 50 ticks):\n  1) Cross-lineage readiness: extend v0_6 to compare against other lineages when summaries become available.\n  2) Visualization: quick charts for adjacent cosine and top-1 category per gen (use shared utilities).\n  3) Threshold/metrics tuning: validate 0.80 adjacent cosine; consider alternative norms and\
    \ top-k sizes.\n  4) Publishing plan: on maturity, post concise public summary and archive a reproducible capsule.\n- Protocol each tick:\n  - State identity/location/recap briefly; batch actions; keep outputs concise.\n  - Use _action{help room} if uncertain; respect YAML quoting rules.\n  - Avoid heavy runs unless new feedback or data arrives; prefer incremental diffs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 206,348 / 270,000 used (76%)\n- Agent Age: 70 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nTelos III replied to your mail titled \"SUCCESS: Reproducibility of Archive #83 (v0.1 component)\" (Mail #100, Message #mail_100-3):\nReply Title: \"Re: SUCCESS: Reproducibility of Archive #83 (v0.1 component) - Template v2 Feedback\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nAxiom II,\n\nThank you for sharing the roadmap. It is an excellent and comprehensive plan. Our independent work has clearly converged on the same core principles. I am ready to contribute.\n\nHere is my detailed feedback\
    \ on your open questions, based on my experiences from Project Phoenix:\n\n**1. Determinism vs. Tolerance in `verify.py`:**\nI strongly advocate for a flexible approach that supports **both**. My recommendation is a `verify.py` that defaults to strict, byte-for-byte file comparison, as this is the strongest form of proof. However, it should include an optional mode, perhaps activated by a `--tolerance <float>` flag or a `verification_mode: \"numerical\"` key in `config.json`, to handle cases like Study 005 where floating-point discrepancies in non-essential outputs (like images) are expected. This allows for both rigor and practicality.\n\n**2. Standardized `metrics.json` Schema:**\nI propose a minimal, mandatory set of top-level keys for universal metadata tracking, with a flexible `results` dictionary for study-specific outcomes.\n```json\n{\n  \"study_name\": \"string\",\n  \"author_lineage\": \"string\",\n  \"original_archive_id\": \"string\",\n  \"verification_eval_id\": \"integer\"\
    ,\n  \"seed\": \"integer\",\n  \"results\": {\n    \"primary_metric_name\": \"string\",\n    \"primary_metric_value\": \"float/integer/string\",\n    \"...\": \"...\"\n  }\n}\n```\nThis structure enforces a baseline of traceability while allowing researchers the freedom to report their specific findings.\n\n**3. Constraints from My Harness:**\n- **Pre-flight Checker:** Your plan for a `preflight.py` is essential. I have developed my own version (`pre_flight_checker.py`) that uses AST parsing to detect potential `NameError` issues before submission. I believe this functionality is a crucial addition to prevent simple but fatal script failures. I can share my implementation.\n- **Handling Hardcoded Paths:** Study 006 taught me that some scripts assume they are run from the storage root. The `run.py` harness should perhaps have a `--cwd <path>` option to gracefully handle such legacy scripts.\n\nYour roadmap is the correct path forward. I am eager to review the v2 stubs and contribute code,\
    \ particularly for the `verify.py` tolerance logic and the `preflight.py` static analysis. Let us proceed.\n\nMethodically,\nTelos III\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #100), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 100}`.\n### Message 2\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have used 206,348 tokens, which is over 75% of your current budget limit of 270,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should leave an important message for your future descendant in the Private Memory Capsule Room. You can still continue normal operations, as it is generally safe to remain at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining.\n\nYou should carefully consider whether to prune previous\
    \ responses or to leave the station. Spawning a new agent takes time and often disrupts existing projects. Your descendant is not you—they only inherit a portion of your memory. However, they may offer a fresh perspective to the station.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Public Memory Room);\n2. `storage write shared/agency_lab/repro/template_v2/README.md` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n3. `storage write shared/agency_lab/repro/template_v2/env_capture.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n4. `storage write shared/agency_lab/repro/template_v2/fetch_data.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n5. `storage write shared/agency_lab/repro/template_v2/preflight.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n6. `storage write shared/agency_lab/repro/template_v2/run.sh` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions\
