  \ on file creation. I'll also consider creating a README.md with quickstart instructions and possibly a helper utils module for SHA256 computation. We can keep things minimal by implementing within run.py and verify.py instead.\n\n**Planning file creation**\n\nI can create multiple files one after the other and log a new private session (#180) to note that we filled in the missing files. After that, I might check the listing again. Sending a quick email to Telos III to inform them about the file updates isn't essential, though. The focus should be on action, so I plan to write several files in the Research Counter. This includes a README.md for descriptions and features, and run.py that captures the environment, reads config paths, fetches data deterministically, and allows user commands.\n\n**Setting up CLI and file structure**\n\nI need to provide CLI arguments like --cwd, --config, and --entrypoint. The preflight_static.py script will validate the verify_config YAML structure by checking\
  \ for required keys in env/data/outputs and ensuring referenced file directories exist. I might even run importlib for static syntax checks.\n\nFor verify.py, I’ll implement \"strict\" and \"with-tolerance\" modes to calculate differences and produce a JSON report with file hashes. I’ll also create utils.py with functions like sha256sum and download_file, though I might embed some utilities directly into each file. To keep things organized, I'll create a common module named utils_v2.py and write files inside the shared directory.\n\n**Implementing content for utils_v2.py**\n\nI’m focusing on creating utils_v2.py where I’ll import necessary libraries like os, sys, json, and hashlib. I’ll implement functions to compute the SHA256 of files, ensure directories exist, and load configurations. Since YAML might not be available, I'll ensure a fallback to JSON loading instead. For efficient downloading, I’ll use urllib.request.urlretrieve, along with the logic to capture the environment setup\
  \ into a manifest.\n\nFor preflight_static.py, I’ll verify the config structure and check that the required output paths and entrypoints exist.\n\nLastly, I’ll set up verify.py to compute file hashes and verify metrics. Additionally, I’ll create the README.md with instructions and track the code uploads with a new session log (#180). Throughout the implementation, I'll make sure to manage token usage effectively.\n\n**Implementing functionality in Python scripts**\n\nI’m working on several scripts. In `utils_v2.py`, I'll create functions for SHA256 checks, directory management, and JSON configuration loading, while ensuring we can also reference both YAML and JSON formats if needed. For `run.py`, I’ll set up command-line arguments and ensure the output directories and files are created correctly.\n\n`preflight_static.py` will validate required keys in the config, checking URLs and paths. Then, in `verify.py`, I’ll manage strict and tolerance modes for verifying outputs and metrics. I'll\
  \ also document the process in the README and log the session for reference. This process aims to create a robust template that’s user-friendly and functional.\n\n**Preparing code files and documentation**\n\nI’m focused on finalizing the structure of my JSON metrics and ensuring it has top-level keys matching metric names. I’ll create a simple system for values and paths in the metrics file to minimize errors. For the `README.md`, I’ll outline quickstart commands. \n\nNext, I will store the necessary Python files in the designated path and verify their upload. I'll make sure to log the session and maintain relevant updates, keeping everything streamlined and easy to navigate for users."
