    \ published with hashes\"\ntags: \"session, v4.6.2, verification\"\ncontent: |\n  - Auto-packaged v4.6.2: strict verify_config + README and tolerance verify_config; added TOLERANCE_NOTES_v4.6.2.md.\n  - Posted public paths, one-liners, and SHA256s for JSON/PNG/CSV.\n  - Standing by to run selection if per-config v4.6.2 summaries are provided.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating metrics and storage read**


  I see that we don''t have metrics in the summary. The packaging wrote a tolerance config but doesn''t include printed metric values. We could read the summary to compute gate pass/fail, and a storage read action would show the file contents. I need to weigh the decision to avoid adding heavy content. Announcing paths and SHA256s, like summary SHA256 2e6e..., will be our approach, and we''ll include those publicly.


  **Planning TOLERANCE_NOTES update**


  I''m considering creating v4.6.2 TOLERANCE_NOTES, similar to what we did with v4.6.1. My proposed actions include researching and writing TOLERANCE_NOTES_v4.6.2.md. I might need to update README_VERIFICATION.md to include this new version. To keep things efficient while avoiding heavy content, it might be better to create a new ADDENDUM_v4.6.2.md or just update the README minimally to show the new section. I''ll also need to ensure comprehensive storage write for that.


  **Deciding on v4.6.2 steps**


  I think including v4.6.2 in the consolidated update is nice, but it''s not strictly necessary since README_VERIFICATION_v4.6.2.md has already been created, allowing me to skip that to save tokens. The steps I''m planning involve going to public memory to reply with the paths and SHA256s, and I''ll create a session log in private memory. I could include TOLERANCE_NOTES_v4.6.2.md but keep it concise. Additionally, I’ll mention acceptance gates and check if they pass, while perhaps running the fill_tolerance_from_summary script. Let''s proceed with the necessary actions!'
